Commands are managed by `dls::CommandManager`. You can dynamically add commands
to the `CommandManager` and it will take care of registering the commands
dynamically with the rest of the framework. When the manager goes out of scope,
it removes all of its commands from the framework. Consider the following simple
example:

```cpp
@EXAMPLE_COMMAND_MAIN_CPP@
```

While this program is running, a user can call these commands from the
framework's console. When the program exits, these commands will automatically
be removed from the console.

Try running this program in one terminal, then in another type:

```cpp
dls -lconsole
```

And then type, for instance:

```txt
foo
bar 1
bar 32
```

The first terminal will run the functions and show its printout.
